186045417] [17592186069725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069918 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthe $x$-axis, and the vertical line $x=2$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/35/35633369d6f4a31ae4c62bb8830dafd9a849aefe434286e2dbf071b91539ccd6.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/e2/e214876a70bdce94316e72d362ebb985bf8e20eaecb90fc427b33c814e647d70.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe method corresponding to the formula above is called the **disc method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin discs (or cylinders) of height $\\Delta{x}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/0214ae60481f0bfa5d1fee7526b6fba48dfe1ee3cba39329e790ca1db79f5dc4.png)\n\nWhen $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$, the radius of each disc can be considered equal to $y=f(x)$. So, the volume of the disc (cylinder) is\n\n$$\n\\pi y^2 \\Delta{x} = \\pi \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\Delta{x}\n$$\n\nIntegrating this expression from $a$ to $b$, we obtain the formula for the volume of the solid.\n\nIn our example above, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nso the volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi\\int_0^2 (\\sqrt{x})^2\\,\\textrm d x = \\pi\\int_0^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= π(\\frac{2^{2}}{2} - \\frac{0^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $2\\pi$ cubic units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069918 #edb/ref 1065 1049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069918 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7fb01e7d-02e7-509a-9164-3bc3410ec61b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069918 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069919 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=y^2$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal line $y=1$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/63/63af72dd7ad6540e418a0947f9c4934a05ade6257904f9eb63733d035a776de8.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis, we again get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/67/67896ee0cc1445194000c0415bbd4453fe93d7cc136cc7479a32df3205d71b5a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same *disc method* approach that we used earlier for the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/27/2730269b09e2019c3f45e48a9dc7849260426dd7ecf1103da74f62f04280f902.png)\n\nThe only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$. The radius of each disc (cylinder) is equal to $x=g(y)$ and its height is $\\Delta{y}$. So, the volume of the disc (cylinder) is\n\n$$\n\\pi x^2 \\Delta{y} = \\pi [ g(y) ]^2 \\Delta{y}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to integrate this expression. Therefore, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $x=g(y)$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{c}^{d} \\left[ g(y) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our example above, we have $x=y^2$, so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{0}^{1}[y^{2}]^{2}dy = π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{4}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{4}dy \\\\\n&= π \\cdot \\left.\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069919 #edb/ref 1065 1051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069919 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "003d7555-c1b0-563e-bd03-4ef97f59c338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069919 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070032 #edb/ref 1064 "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}x & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}x(\\operatorname{ln}x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070032 #edb/ref 1065 904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070032 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eac7b2d2-bdab-56e5-bb03-00e69bb0caf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070032 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070036 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070036 #edb/ref 1065 922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "de271889-71f4-5fa2-8346-7ed8fa03e000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070036 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078483 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186320789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 